WEBVTT

00:01.350 --> 00:03.806
Every time we sit, lean, or breathe,

00:03.846 --> 00:06.576
we gently deform the surfaces that hold us.

00:06.923 --> 00:11.568
Those tiny deformations carry signals
about posture, and even breathing.

00:11.729 --> 00:16.700
But they are hard to see without
instrumenting the surface, or the person.

00:17.250 --> 00:19.713
Moiré patterns can make them visible.

00:19.804 --> 00:22.423
When two fine, repeating patterns overlap,

00:22.463 --> 00:26.296
a tiny shift between them turns
into a large, sweeping fringe.

00:26.642 --> 00:30.120
And woven surfaces, like
the mesh of an office chair,

00:30.160 --> 00:33.018
already carry that repeating pattern.

00:35.268 --> 00:38.381
MoiréDeform turns that mesh into a sensor.

00:38.542 --> 00:40.615
A low-cost camera behind the chair

00:40.655 --> 00:43.687
records one reference frame
of the empty backrest.

00:44.034 --> 00:46.432
Each live frame is compared against it,

00:46.472 --> 00:48.754
so the two act as virtual layers,

00:48.794 --> 00:51.733
and deformation appears as a moiré pattern.

00:51.894 --> 00:56.516
No markers, no added gratings,
no changes to the chair.

00:57.166 --> 00:59.808
The camera sees only the back of the chair.

00:59.848 --> 01:03.182
The person sitting in it is never in frame.

01:04.432 --> 01:08.416
In the first demonstration,
the user simply sits and breathes.

01:08.507 --> 01:11.179
Each breath presses gently into the mesh.

01:11.270 --> 01:14.098
From the moiré signal,
a lightweight neural network

01:14.138 --> 01:16.605
reconstructs the breathing waveform live,

01:16.645 --> 01:20.989
shown here against a chest belt
that is worn only for comparison.

01:21.439 --> 01:23.199
When the user holds their breath,

01:23.239 --> 01:26.897
the system flags the hold within
about one and a half seconds.

01:27.058 --> 01:31.402
And when breathing resumes,
the waveform picks right back up.

01:33.452 --> 01:35.985
Larger deformations become input.

01:36.447 --> 01:41.237
Shifting your weight left, right,
up, or down across the backrest

01:41.277 --> 01:43.253
moves the load on the mesh.

01:43.855 --> 01:46.346
The weighted centroid of the moiré envelope

01:46.386 --> 01:50.207
turns that shift into a continuous,
two-dimensional joystick.

01:50.460 --> 01:53.938
Here, it guides a seated back-mobility exercise,

01:53.978 --> 01:56.511
steered by posture alone.

01:57.561 --> 02:02.119
In a preliminary study with five users
and one hundred minutes of recordings,

02:02.159 --> 02:04.127
MoiréDeform tracked breathing rate

02:04.167 --> 02:08.348
within about 0.7 breaths
per minute of a chest belt.

02:08.440 --> 02:10.966
And with the same camera, the moiré signal

02:11.006 --> 02:14.850
was about three times more accurate
than tracking a physical marker:

02:15.104 --> 02:21.131
0.71 versus 2.07 breaths per minute of error.

02:21.981 --> 02:25.029
The same channel could support
stretch-break coaching,

02:25.069 --> 02:27.665
posture awareness through a long workday,

02:27.705 --> 02:31.201
hands-free input, or games you play by leaning.

02:31.548 --> 02:34.742
All of it by watching fabric, not faces.

02:34.903 --> 02:37.138
When everyday surfaces can sense,

02:37.178 --> 02:40.025
every chair becomes a private interface.
